---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import FlipImage from '@/components/tools/image-edit/FlipImage.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="flip-image">
  <FlipImage client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to flip an image</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop one or more images onto the box above.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>horizontal</strong> to mirror left ↔ right, <strong>vertical</strong> to mirror top ↕ bottom, or both.</li>
      <li>Check the live preview of the first image, then press <strong>Flip</strong> and download the files or the ZIP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Flip, mirror, rotate: the difference</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>What happens</th><th>Text readable?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Flip horizontal</td><td>Reflected across the vertical centre line</td><td>No: reads backwards</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flip vertical</td><td>Reflected across the horizontal centre line</td><td>No: upside down and reversed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flip both</td><td>Identical to a 180° rotation</td><td>No: upside down</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/rotate-image">Rotate 180°</a></td><td>Turned, not reflected</td><td>No: upside down</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="/rotate-image">Rotate 90°</a></td><td>Turned a quarter turn</td><td>Yes, sideways</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The important property is chirality. Another rotation can always undo a rotation; no rotation can
      undo a single flip. A flip swaps left and right, so a flipped photo of a shop front has unreadable
      signs, and a flipped portrait has the parting on the wrong side.
    </p>

    <h2>When flipping is the right tool</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Selfies: phone cameras mirror the front-camera preview like a bathroom mirror, then save the true view. A horizontal flip gives back the version you composed on screen.</li>
      <li>Scanned negatives and slides: film loaded emulsion side down scans reversed; a horizontal flip corrects it.</li>
      <li>Layout balance: editors often mirror a subject so it faces into the page and not off the edge.</li>
      <li>Iron-on transfers and heat-press printing: mirror these before printing, so they read correctly after application.</li>
      <li>Sprite sheets and game art: make a left-facing version from a right-facing one.</li>
      <li>Documents scanned upside down: flip both axes, which is the same as a 180° rotation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Is any quality lost?</h2>
    <p>
      No. A flip only moves pixels: each value is copied without change to a new position, with no
      interpolation, averaging or rounding. The exported file is pixel-identical to a mirrored copy of the
      original. The only exception is the encode step: JPEG in and JPEG out means one more compression.
      Export PNG or WebP for a perfect round trip, or keep the quality at 90 or more, where the difference is
      invisible.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on text and logos</h2>
    <p>
      A flip reverses all writing in the frame. If the image has a logo, a licence plate, a street sign or a
      watermark that must stay legible, flip the background separately and add the text afterwards. The
      <a href="/watermark-image">watermark tool</a> can put it on the flipped result the correct way round.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
